ns have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1065 2768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321160 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321452 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321452 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321452 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322153 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322153 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322153 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322348 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323072 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323072 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323072 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186322153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323253 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\nI. $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n\nII. $x\\in [-2,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
